 "endReason": "concluded",
 "customerTurns": 5,
 "transcript": [
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "my washin mashine wont turn on at all no lights nothin"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "A washing machine with absolutely no lights or response usually means it isn't receiving mains electricity. The most common cause is simply that the wall socket is dead (perhaps due to a tripped RCD or fuse) or the plug/lead isn't making good contact. Please check if the socket actually has power by plugging in another device, such as a lamp. If it's on a switched spur unit, make sure the switch is turned on. Let me know what happens when you test that."
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "the kettle works in the kitchen so the sockets are ok i think. the washer is built in behind a cupboard door so i cant see the plug. what do i do?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "If the kettle works in that room but the washing machine is completely dead, the problem is likely either a switched socket you haven't turned on, or an issue with the plug/cable itself (which can fail even if it looks okay). Since it's built-in, please pull the machine out just enough to see the back. Check for a wall switch near the cupboard and make sure it's on. If there isn't one, unplug the machine and inspect the plug pins for burning or the cable for damage where it enters the machine.\n[Panel: \"To check a suitable replacement — Enter your model number below. Once we have the model I can check whether a suitable replacement is available. It's usually on a sticker inside the door or around the frame, or on the back of the appliance.\"]"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "i cant pull it out its built in and im not allowed to take the cupboard off, and i dont have any tools. i dont know the model either, the name on the door edge is worn off. is there a switch somewhere i can check instead?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Yes, there often is. Look for a small red or black switch on the wall near the cupboard (sometimes behind a hinged cover) and make sure it's pressed to 'I' or 'on'. If you don't see one nearby, check your main fuse box or consumer unit to see if any circuit breaker has tripped."
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "ok i looked and theres a row of switches above the worktop and one says washer and its off. my flatmate must have switched it off when cleaning. should i turn it on?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "[Heading: Likely cause: power switch]\nYes, please turn that switch on. That is the dedicated isolation switch for your washing machine, and it is very common for it to be switched off after cleaning or maintenance. Once you've turned it back on (usually to the 'I' position), check if the lights come on and try running a short programme. If it still doesn't respond after switching it on, let me know."
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "ok i turned it on and the machine lights up now and works! thanks"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "[Heading: Likely cause: power switch]\nThat's excellent news! It was simply the dedicated isolation switch behind the worktop that had been turned off. To prevent this from happening again, you might want to leave a small note near that switch for your flatmate so they know not to switch it off when cleaning. Is there anything else I can help you with today?"
  }
 ],
